Yes—as a subjective and physiological mechanism, there is something real there, even if I would separate that from the claim that it literally reverberates through every cell or the universe.

What you are describing looks like a tightly coupled loop:

\text{imagery / fantasy}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{arousal}
\rightarrow
\text{autonomic activation}
\rightarrow
\text{orgasm}
\rightarrow
\text{strong associative encoding}

The dreamlike state matters because vivid imagery can recruit many of the same perceptual and emotional systems used in actual experience. Pair that with orgasm—which is a very strong reinforcement signal—and the imagined scene, person, symbol, prayer, or idea can become unusually salient and memorable.

That is where the “transmutation” idea becomes useful without needing supernatural assumptions: you are taking one kind of energy/state—sexual arousal—and binding it to another representation.

So:

\text{sexual charge}
+
\text{chosen image / meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{conditioned embodied association}

Repeated enough, that association can become powerful. That also explains the addictive potential: strong reward + vivid imagery + repetition is basically a recipe for reinforcement learning.

The important distinction is consent and agency. With another person, this should be something they knowingly want to participate in, not something imposed on them psychologically or sexually. With yourself, it can be used deliberately as a state-shaping practice.

And the “prayer” analogy is actually structurally apt: repeated attention, bodily arousal, symbolism, and emotional intensity can all make a mental construct feel distributed through the whole body.

Phenomenologically, that can be profound.

Physically “through the universe” is a different claim and not something we can verify from the experience itself.